#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE FIFTH STAGE

Now, as Christian went on his way, he came to a little rise, which had been thrown up on purpose so that pilgrims could see the way ahead of them. So Christian went up there; and looking forward, he saw Faithful ahead of him on his journey. Then Christian called out loud, Ho, ho! Hello there! Wait, and I will be your companion. At that Faithful looked back, and Christian called to him again, Wait, wait, till I catch up with you. But Faithful answered, No, I am running for my life, and the avenger of blood is behind me.

At this Christian was somewhat stung, and putting out all his strength, he quickly caught up with Faithful, and even ran past him; so the last was first. Then Christian smiled vaingloriously, because he had got ahead of his brother; but not watching his feet carefully, he suddenly stumbled and fell, and could not get up again until Faithful came up to help him.

Then I saw in my dream that they went on together very lovingly, and talked sweetly of all the things that had happened to them on their pilgrimage; and Christian began like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: My honored and dearly loved brother Faithful, I am glad that I have caught up with you, and that God has so attuned our spirits that we can walk as companions on so pleasant a path.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I had expected, my dear friend, to have your company all the way from our town, but you got a head start on me; so I was forced to come this much of the way alone.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: How long did you stay in the city of Destruction before you set out after me on your pilgrimage?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Until I could stay no longer; for soon after you left there was a great deal of talk that our city would shortly be burned to the ground with fire from heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What, did your neighbors talk like that?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, for a while it was on everybody’s lips.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What, and did nobody but you come out to escape the danger?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Though there was, as I said, a great deal of talk about it, I do not think they firmly believed it; for in the heat of the talk I heard some of them speak mockingly of you and of your desperate journey, which is what they called this pilgrimage of yours. But I believed, and still do, that our city will come to its end by fire and brimstone from above; and so I have made my escape.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you not hear any talk of neighbor Pliable?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, Christian, I heard that he followed you until he came to the Slough of Despond, where, as some said, he fell in; but he did not want it known that he had done so. Still, I am sure he was thoroughly spattered with that kind of dirt.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did the neighbors say to him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Since he went back he has been held in great scorn, and that among all sorts of people: some mock and despise him, and hardly anyone will give him work. He is now seven times worse than if he had never gone out of the city.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But why should they be so set against him, since they despise the way that he abandoned as well?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Oh, they say, Hang him; he is a turncoat; he was not true to his profession of faith! I think God has stirred up even His enemies to hiss at him and make him a byword, because he has abandoned the way. Jer 29:18, 19.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you not talk with him before you came out?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I met him once in the streets, but he slunk away on the other side, like a man ashamed of what he had done; so I did not speak to him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, when I first set out I had hopes of that man; but now I fear he will perish when the city is overthrown. For it has happened to him just as the true proverb says, The dog has turned to his own vomit again, and the sow that was washed to her wallowing in the mire. 2 Pet 2:22.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Those are my fears for him too; but who can stop what will be?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, neighbor Faithful, Christian said, let us leave him, and talk of things that concern ourselves more closely. Tell me now what you have met with on the way as you came; for I know you have met with some things, or else it should be written down as a wonder.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I escaped the slough that I see you fell into, and got up to the gate without that danger; only I met one whose name was Wanton, who nearly did me harm.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: It was a good thing you escaped her net. Joseph was hard pressed by her, and he escaped her as you did; but it nearly cost him his life. Gen 39:11–13. But what did she do to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: You cannot imagine (unless you know something of it already) what a flattering tongue she had; she pressed me hard to turn aside with her, promising me every kind of satisfaction.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: No, she did not promise you the satisfaction of a good conscience.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: You know what I mean; all carnal and fleshly satisfaction.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Thank God you escaped her: the abhorred of the Lord shall fall into her pit. Prov 22:14.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, I do not know whether I escaped her completely or not.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, surely you did not consent to her desires?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, not to defile myself; for I remembered an old writing that I had seen, which said, “Her steps lead straight to Hell.” Prov 5:5. So I shut my eyes, so that I would not be bewitched by her looks. Job 31:1. Then she shouted abuse at me, and I went my way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you meet no other attack as you came?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: When I came to the foot of the hill called Difficulty, I met a very old man, who asked me who I was and where I was going. I told him that I was a pilgrim, going to the Celestial City. Then the old man said, You look like an honest fellow; would you be willing to live with me for the wages that I will give you? Then I asked his name, and where he lived. He said his name was Adam the First, and that he lived in the town of Deceit. Eph 4:22. I asked him then what his work was, and what wages he would give. He told me that his work was #emph[many delights;] and his wages, that I would be his heir at last. I further asked him what sort of house he kept, and what other servants he had. So he told me that his house was stocked with all the delicacies of the world, and that his servants were those he had fathered himself. Then I asked how many children he had. He said that he had only three daughters, the Lust of the Flesh, the Lust of the Eyes, and the Pride of Life, 1 John 2:16; and that I could marry them if I wanted to. Then I asked how long he wanted me to live with him. And he told me, as long as he lived himself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, and what conclusion did you and the old man come to in the end?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Why, at first I found myself somewhat inclined to go with the man, for I thought he spoke very persuasively; but looking at his forehead as I talked with him, I saw written there, “Put off the old man with his deeds.”]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Then it came burning hot into my mind that, whatever he said and however he flattered, once he got me home to his house he would sell me as a slave. So I told him to stop talking, for I would not come near the door of his house. Then he heaped insults on me, and told me that he would send someone after me who would make my way bitter to my soul. So I turned to go away from him; but just as I turned to leave, I felt him take hold of my flesh and give me such a deadly wrench backward that I thought he had pulled part of me away with him. This made me cry, “O wretched man.” Rom 7:24. So I went on my way up the hill.]

Now, when I had got more than halfway up, I looked back and saw someone coming after me, as swift as the wind; and he overtook me just about where the bench stands.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Just there, Christian said, I sat down to rest; but I was overcome with sleep, and there I lost this roll out of my bosom.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But, good brother, hear me out. As soon as the man overtook me, it was only a word and a blow; for he knocked me down and left me for dead. But when I had come to myself a little, I asked him why he treated me like that. He said it was because of my secret leaning toward Adam the First. And with that he struck me another deadly blow on the chest, and beat me down backward; so I lay at his feet as dead as before. So when I came to myself again I begged him for mercy; but he said, I do not know how to show mercy; and with that he knocked me down again. He would certainly have finished me off, if someone had not come by and told him to stop.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Who was it that told him to stop?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I did not know him at first; but as he went by, I saw the holes in his hands and in his side, and then I concluded that he was our Lord. So I went up the hill.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That man who overtook you was Moses. He spares no one, nor does he know how to show mercy to those who transgress the law.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I know it very well; it was not the first time he has met me. It was he who came to me when I lived complacently at home, and who told me he would burn my house down over my head if I stayed there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But did you not see the house that stood at the top of that hill, where Moses met you on the slope?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, and the lions too, before I reached it. But as for the lions, I think they were asleep, for it was about noon; and because I had so much of the day still ahead of me, I passed by the Porter, and came down the hill.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He did tell me that he saw you go by; but I wish you had called at the house, for they would have shown you so many rare things that you would hardly have forgotten them to your dying day. But please tell me, did you meet nobody in the Valley of Humility?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, I met one Discontent, who would gladly have persuaded me to go back with him. His reason was that the valley was altogether without honor. He told me, moreover, that going there was the way to alienate all my friends, such as Pride, Arrogancy, Self-Conceit, Worldly Glory, and others, who, he said, he knew would be very much offended if I made such a fool of myself as to wade through this valley.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, and how did you answer him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I told him that although all these he named might claim to be my kin, and rightly so (for indeed they were my relatives according to the flesh), yet since I became a pilgrim they have disowned me, and I have rejected them too; and so they were now no more to me than if they had never been of my family line. I told him, moreover, that as for this valley, he had quite misrepresented it; for before honor is humility, and a haughty spirit before a fall. Therefore, I said, I would rather go through this valley to what the wisest counted honor, than choose what he valued as most worthy of our affections.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Did you meet nothing else in that valley?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Yes, I met Shame; but of all the men I met on my pilgrimage, he, I think, bears the wrong name. The others would take no for an answer after a little argument and one thing or another; but this bold-faced Shame would never give up.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, what did he say to you?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: What? Why, he objected to religion itself. He said it was a pitiful, low, cringing business for a man to care about religion. He said that a tender conscience was an unmanly thing; and that for a man to watch over his words and ways, so as to tie himself down from the swaggering liberty that the brave spirits of the times were used to, would make him the laughingstock of the age. He also objected that only a few of the mighty, rich, or wise were ever of my opinion; nor any of them either, until they had been persuaded to be fools, and to be so willingly foolish as to risk the loss of all for nobody knows what. 1 Cor 1:26; 3:18; Phil 3:7–9; John 7:48. He objected, moreover, to the poor and low estate and condition of those who were, for the most part, the pilgrims of the times in which they lived; and also to their ignorance and lack of understanding in all natural science. Yes, he pressed me hard in that way about a great many more things than I relate here: such as, that it was a shame to sit whining and mourning under a sermon, and a shame to come home sighing and groaning; that it was a shame to ask my neighbor’s forgiveness for petty faults, or to make restitution where I have taken from anyone. He said also that religion made a man grow distant from the great, because of a few vices (which he called by finer names), and made him acknowledge and respect the lowly, because they were of the same religious brotherhood. And is this not, he said, a shame?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: And what did you say to him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Say? I could not tell what to say at first. Indeed, he pressed me so hard that my blood rushed up into my face; it was this Shame that brought it up, and he had almost beaten me off altogether. But at last I began to consider that what is highly valued among men is held in abomination by God. Luke 16:15. And I thought again, this Shame tells me what men are; but he tells me nothing about what God, or the word of God, is. And I thought, moreover, that at the day of doom we shall not be doomed to death or life according to the swaggering spirits of the world, but according to the wisdom and law of the Highest. Therefore, I thought, what God says is best is indeed best, though all the men in the world are against it. Seeing, then, that God prefers his religion; seeing God prefers a tender conscience; seeing those who make themselves fools for the kingdom of heaven are wisest, and that the poor man who loves Christ is richer than the greatest man in the world who hates him: Shame, depart; you are an enemy to my salvation. Shall I welcome you against my sovereign Lord? How then could I look him in the face at his coming? Mark 8:38. If I were ashamed of his ways and servants now, how could I expect the blessing? But indeed this Shame was a bold villain; I could hardly shake him out of my company; he kept on haunting me, and continually whispering in my ear about one or another of the weaknesses that go with religion. But at last I told him that it was useless to try any further in this business; for the very things he despised were the things in which I saw the most glory. And so at last I got past this persistent fellow. And when I had shaken him off, I began to sing,]

#quote[“The trials that those men do meet withal, \
That are obedient to the heavenly call, \
Are manifold, and suited to the flesh, \
And come, and come, and come again afresh; \
That now, or some time else, we by them may \
Be taken, overcome, and cast away. \
O let the pilgrims, let the pilgrims then, \
Be vigilant, and quit themselves like men.”]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: I am glad, my brother, that you stood up to this villain so bravely; for of them all, as you say, I think he has the wrong name; for he is so bold as to follow us in the streets, and to try to put us to shame before all men; that is, to make us ashamed of what is good. But if he were not so audacious himself, he would never attempt to do what he does. But let us go on resisting him; for, in spite of all his bravado, he promotes the fool, and no one else. “The wise will inherit glory,” said Solomon, “but shame will be the promotion of fools.” Prov 3:35.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: I think we must cry for help against Shame to Him who would have us be valiant for truth on the earth.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You are right; but did you meet nobody else in that valley?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, I did not; for I had sunshine all the rest of the way through that valley, and through the Valley of the Shadow of Death as well.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You were fortunate; I am sure it went very differently with me. For a long time, almost as soon as I entered that valley, I had a dreadful fight with that foul fiend Apollyon; indeed, I truly thought he would kill me, especially when he got me down and crushed me under him, as if he would crush me to pieces; for as he threw me, my sword flew out of my hand. He even told me he was sure of me; but I cried to God, and he heard me, and delivered me out of all my troubles. Then I entered the Valley of the Shadow of Death, and had no light for almost half the way through it. I thought I would be killed there over and over; but at last day broke, and the sun rose, and I went through the rest with far more ease and quiet.]

Moreover, I saw in my dream that as they went on, Faithful, happening to look to one side, saw a man whose name was Talkative, walking at a distance beside them; for in this place there was room enough for them all to walk. He was a tall man, and rather better-looking at a distance than close up. Faithful turned to this man and spoke to him like this.

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Friend, where are you headed? Are you going to the heavenly country?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I am going to the same place.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: That is good; then I hope we will have your good company?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I will be your companion very gladly.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Come on, then, let us go together, and let us spend our time talking about things that are profitable.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: To talk of things that are good is very welcome to me, with you or with anyone else; and I am glad that I have met people who are inclined to so good a work; for, to tell the truth, there are only a few who care to spend their time like this as they travel; most would much rather talk of things of no profit; and this has troubled me.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: That is indeed something to be lamented; for what is so worthy of the use of men’s tongues and mouths on earth as the things of the God of heaven?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I like you wonderfully well, for what you say is very convincing; and I will add, What is so pleasant, and what so profitable, as to talk of the things of God? What things are so pleasant? That is, if a man has any delight in things that are wonderful. For instance, if a man delights to talk of the history or the mystery of things; or if a man loves to talk of miracles, wonders, or signs, where will he find things recorded so delightfully, and so sweetly written, as in the holy Scripture?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: That is true; but our chief aim in our talk should be to profit from such things.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: That is just what I said; for to talk of such things is most profitable; for by doing so a man may gain knowledge of many things, such as the vanity of earthly things and the benefit of things above. That is in general; but more particularly, by this a man may learn the necessity of the new birth, the insufficiency of our works, the need of Christ’s righteousness, etc. Besides, by this a man may learn what it is to repent, to believe, to pray, to suffer, and the like; by this, also, a man may learn what the great promises and consolations of the Gospel are, to his own comfort. Further, by this a man may learn to refute false opinions, to defend the truth, and also to instruct the ignorant.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: All this is true; and I am glad to hear these things from you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Alas! the lack of this is the reason why so few understand the need of faith, and the necessity of a work of grace in their soul in order to have eternal life; instead they ignorantly live in the works of the law, by which a man can by no means obtain the kingdom of heaven.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But, if I may say so, heavenly knowledge of these things is the gift of God; no man attains it by human effort, or only by talking about them.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: All this I know very well; for a man can receive nothing unless it is given him from heaven: all is of grace, not of works. I could give you a hundred scriptures to confirm this.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, then, Faithful said, what is the one thing that we should base our talk on at this time?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Whatever you like. I will talk of things heavenly or things earthly; things moral or things evangelical; things sacred or things profane; things past or things to come; things foreign or things at home; things more essential or things incidental: provided that it is all done to our profit.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Now Faithful began to marvel; and stepping over to Christian (for Christian had been walking by himself all this while), he said to him, but softly, What a fine companion we have got! Surely this man will make a very excellent pilgrim.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: At this Christian smiled modestly, and said, This man you are so taken with will deceive, with this tongue of his, twenty people who do not know him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Do you know him, then?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Know him? Yes, better than he knows himself.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Then tell me, who is he?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: His name is Talkative; he lives in our town. I am surprised that you should be a stranger to him, except that I remember that our town is large.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Whose son is he? And whereabouts does he live?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: He is the son of one Say-well. He lived in Prating-Row; and he is known to all who are acquainted with him as Talkative of Prating-Row; and in spite of his fine tongue, he is nothing but a sorry fellow.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, he seems to be a very fine man.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: That is, to those who do not know him thoroughly, for he is best away from home; near home he is ugly enough. Your saying that he is a fine man brings to mind what I have noticed in the work of a painter, whose pictures look best at a distance, but close up are less pleasing.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But I am inclined to think you are only joking, because you smiled.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: God forbid that I should joke (though I smiled) about this matter, or that I should accuse anyone falsely. I will show you more of what he is. This man is for any company and any talk; as he talks now with you, so he will talk when he is on the ale-bench; and the more drink he has in his head, the more of these things he has in his mouth. Religion has no place in his heart, or house, or life; all he has lies in his tongue, and his religion is to make a noise with it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Is that so? Then I have been badly deceived in this man.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Deceived! You may be sure of it. Remember the proverb, “They say, and do not”; but the kingdom of God is not in word, but in power. Matt 23:3; 1 Cor 4:20. He talks of prayer, of repentance, of faith, and of the new birth; but he only knows how to talk of them. I have been in his family, and have watched him both at home and away from home; and I know that what I say of him is the truth. His house is as empty of religion as the white of an egg is of taste. There is neither prayer there nor any sign of repentance for sin; indeed, the brute, in its own way, serves God far better than he does. He is the very stain, reproach, and shame of religion to all who know him, Rom 2:24, 25; because of him, religion can hardly get a good word in all that end of the town where he lives. So the common people who know him say, “A saint abroad, and a devil at home.” His poor family finds it so; he is such a boor, so abusive to his servants, and so unreasonable with them, that they neither know how to work for him nor how to speak to him. Men who have any dealings with him say, It is better to deal with a Turk than with him, for they will get fairer dealing at the Turks’ hands. This Talkative (if it is possible) will outwit them, defraud, deceive, and overreach them. Besides, he brings up his sons to follow in his steps; and if he finds in any of them a foolish timidity (for so he calls the first sign of a tender conscience), he calls them fools and blockheads, and will by no means trust them with much, or speak well of them before others. For my part, I am of the opinion that he has, by his wicked life, caused many to stumble and fall; and he will be, unless God prevents it, the ruin of many more.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, my brother, I am bound to believe you, not only because you say you know him, but also because you report on men as a Christian should. For I cannot think that you say these things out of ill will, but because it is just as you say.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: If I had known him no better than you, I might perhaps have thought of him as you did at first; yes, and if I had heard this report only from those who are enemies of religion, I would have thought it was a slander—a fate that often falls from bad men’s mouths on good men’s names and professions of faith. But all these things, yes, and a great many more just as bad, I can prove him guilty of from my own knowledge. Besides, good men are ashamed of him; they can call him neither brother nor friend; the very mention of his name among them makes them blush, if they know him.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, I see that saying and doing are two things, and from now on I will mark this distinction better.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: They are two things indeed, and are as different as the soul and the body; for as the body without the soul is only a dead carcass, so #emph[saying], if it is alone, is only a dead carcass too. The soul of religion is the practical part. “Pure and undefiled religion before our God and Father is this: to care for orphans and widows in their distress, and to keep oneself from being polluted by the world.” Jas 1:27; see also verses 22-26. Talkative is not aware of this; he thinks that hearing and saying will make a good Christian, and so he deceives his own soul. Hearing is only the sowing of the seed; talking is not enough to prove that fruit is really in the heart and life. And let us be sure that at the day of doom men will be judged according to their fruits. Matt 13:23. It will not be said then, Did you believe? but, Were you doers, or only talkers? and they will be judged accordingly. The end of the world is compared to our harvest, Matt 13:30, and you know that at harvest men care about nothing but fruit. Not that anything can be accepted that is not of faith; but I say this to show you how insignificant Talkative’s profession of faith will be on that day.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: This brings to mind what Moses says when he describes the beast that is clean. Lev 11; Deut 14. It is one that parts the hoof and chews the cud; not one that parts the hoof only, or that chews the cud only. The hare chews the cud, but is still unclean, because he does not part the hoof. And this is a true likeness of Talkative: he chews the cud, he seeks knowledge; he chews on the word, but he does not divide the hoof. He does not part with the way of sinners; but, like the hare, he keeps the foot of the dog or bear, and therefore he is unclean.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You have given, as far as I know, the true gospel sense of these texts. And I will add another thing: Paul calls some men, yes, and great talkers at that, sounding brass and tinkling cymbals, 1 Cor 13:1, 3; that is, as he explains them in another place, things without life that give a sound. 1 Cor 14:7. Things without life; that is, without the true faith and grace of the gospel; and so, things that will never be placed in the kingdom of heaven among those who are the children of life, even though their sound, by their talk, is like the tongue or voice of an angel.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, I was no fonder of his company at first than I am sick of it now. What can we do to get rid of him?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Take my advice, and do as I tell you, and you will find that he will soon be sick of your company too, unless God touches his heart and turns it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: What do you want me to do?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, go to him, and start a serious discussion about the power of religion; and ask him plainly (once he has approved of it, as he will) whether this thing is set up in his heart, house, or life.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Then Faithful stepped forward again, and said to Talkative, Come, how are you doing? How is it going now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Fine, thank you. I thought we would have had a great deal of talk by now.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, if you like, we will get down to it now; and since you left it to me to set the question, let it be this: How does the saving grace of God show itself when it is in the heart of man?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: I see, then, that our talk must be about the power of things. Well, it is a very good question, and I will gladly answer you. Here is my answer in brief: First, where the grace of God is in the heart, it causes a great outcry there against sin. Secondly—]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, wait; let us consider one at a time. I think you should rather say, it shows itself by inclining the soul to abhor its sin.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Why, what difference is there between crying out against sin and abhorring it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Oh! a great deal. A man may cry out against sin out of self-interest; but he cannot abhor it except through a godly hatred of it. I have heard many cry out against sin in the pulpit who can still put up with it well enough in the heart, house, and life. Gen 39:15. Joseph’s mistress cried out with a loud voice, as if she had been very holy; but in spite of that she would gladly have committed uncleanness with him. Some cry out against sin just as a mother cries out against the child in her lap, when she calls it little rascal and naughty girl, and then starts hugging and kissing it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: You are trying to catch me out, I see.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: No, not I; I am only for setting things right. But what is the second thing by which you would prove that there is a work of grace in the heart?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Great knowledge of gospel mysteries.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: This sign should have come first; but first or last, it is also false; for knowledge, great knowledge, may be gained in the mysteries of the Gospel, and yet there may be no work of grace in the soul. Even if a man has all knowledge, he may still be nothing, and so, consequently, be no child of God. 1 Cor 13:2. When Christ said, “Do you#super[+] know all these things?” and the disciples answered, Yes, he added, “You#super[+] will be blessed if you do them.” He does not place the blessing in knowing them, but in doing them. For there is a knowledge that is not followed by doing: “He who knows his Master’s will, and does not do it.” A man may know like an angel, and still be no Christian; so your sign is not true. Indeed, to #emph[know] is a thing that pleases talkers and boasters; but to #emph[do] is what pleases God. Not that the heart can be good without knowledge, for without that the heart is worthless. So there are two sorts of knowledge: knowledge that rests in the bare theory of things, and knowledge that is accompanied by the grace of faith and love, which sets a man to doing even the will of God from the heart. The first of these will serve the talker; but without the other, the true Christian is not content. “Give me understanding that I may obey Your law, and follow it with all my heart.” Ps 119:34.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: You are trying to catch me out again: this is not for edification.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, if you please, put forward another sign of how this work of grace shows itself where it is.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Not I, for I see we will not agree.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Well, if you will not, will you allow me to do it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: You are at liberty to.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: A work of grace in the soul shows itself either to the man who has it, or to those standing by.]

To the man who has it, it shows itself like this: It gives him conviction of sin, especially of the defilement of his nature and of the sin of unbelief, for which he is sure to be damned if he does not find mercy at God’s hand by faith in Jesus Christ. This sight and sense of things works sorrow and shame for sin in him. Ps 38:18; Jer 31:19; John 16:8; Rom 7:24; Mark 16:16; Gal 2:16; Rev 1:6. He finds, moreover, the Savior of the world revealed in him, and the absolute necessity of embracing him for life; and at this he finds hungerings and thirstings after him; and it is to such hungerings, etc., that the promise is made. Now, according to the strength or weakness of his faith in his Savior, so is his joy and peace, so is his love of holiness, so are his desires to know him more, and also to serve him in this world. But though, I say, it shows itself to him in this way, yet he is only seldom able to conclude that this is a work of grace; because his corruptions now, and his abused reason, make his mind misjudge in this matter. So in the man who has this work, a very sound judgment is required before he can firmly conclude that this is a work of grace. John 16:9; Gal 2:15, 16; Acts 4:12; Matt 5:6; Rev 21:6.

To others it is shown like this:

1\. By a confession of his faith in Christ from experience. 2. By a life matching that confession; that is, a life of holiness—heart-holiness, family-holiness (if he has a family), and holiness of conduct in the world; which in general teaches him inwardly to abhor his sin, and himself for it, in secret; to suppress it in his family, and to promote holiness in the world: not by talk only, as a hypocrite or talkative person may do, but by a practical submission in faith and love to the power of the word. Job 42:5, 6; Ps 50:23; Ezek 20:43; Matt 5:8; John 14:15; Rom 10:10; Ezek 36:25; Phil 1:27; 3:17–20. And now, sir, as to this brief description of the work of grace, and also of how it is shown, if you have any objection, make it; if not, then allow me to put a second question to you.

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: No, my part now is not to object but to listen; so let me have your second question.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: It is this: Do you experience this first part of the description; and do your life and conduct bear witness to the same? Or does your religion stand in word or tongue, and not in deed and truth? Please, if you are inclined to answer me on this, say no more than you know the God above will say Amen to, and nothing but what your conscience can justify you in; for it is not the one who commends himself who is approved, but the one whom the Lord commends. Besides, to say I am such and such, when my conduct and all my neighbors tell me I lie, is great wickedness.]

Then Talkative at first began to blush; but, recovering himself, he replied like this: You come now to experience, to conscience, and to God, and appeal to him to justify what is spoken. I did not expect this kind of talk; nor am I disposed to answer such questions, because I do not count myself bound to, unless you take it upon yourself to catechize me; and even if you did, I may still refuse to make you my judge. But please, will you tell me why you ask me such questions?

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: Because I saw you were eager to talk, and because I did not know that you had anything more than notions. Besides, to tell you the whole truth, I have heard of you that you are a man whose religion lies in talk, and that your conduct gives the lie to this profession of your mouth. They say you are a stain among Christians, and that religion fares the worse for your ungodly conduct; that some have already stumbled at your wicked ways, and that more are in danger of being destroyed by them: your religion, and an ale-house, and covetousness, and uncleanness, and swearing, and lying, and keeping vain company, etc., will all stand together. The proverb that is said of a harlot is true of you, namely, “That she is a shame to all women”: so you are a shame to all professing Christians.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Talkative]: Since you are so ready to take up reports and to judge as rashly as you do, I can only conclude that you are some peevish or melancholy man, not fit to talk with; and so, goodbye.]

Then Christian came up and said to his brother, I told you how it would turn out; your words and his lusts could not agree. He would rather leave your company than reform his life. But he is gone, as I said: let him go; the loss is no man’s but his own. He has saved us the trouble of leaving him; for if he went on as he is (as I suppose he will), he would have been nothing but a blot in our company. Besides, the apostle says, “From such withdraw yourself.”

#speech[#smallcaps[Faithful]: But I am glad we had this little talk with him; perhaps he will think about it again. In any case, I have dealt plainly with him, and so I am clear of his blood if he perishes.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: You did well to talk to him as plainly as you did. There is very little of this faithful dealing with men nowadays, and that is what makes religion stink so in the nostrils of many; for it is these talkative fools, whose religion is only in word, and who are debauched and vain in their conduct, who (being admitted so much into the fellowship of the godly) confuse the world, blemish Christianity, and grieve the sincere. I wish that all men would deal with such people as you have done; then they would either be brought more into line with religion, or the company of saints would be too hot for them. Then Faithful said,]

#quote[“How Talkative at first lifts up his plumes! \
How bravely doth he speak! How he presumes \
To drive down all before him! But so soon \
As Faithful talks of heart-work, like the moon \
That’s past the full, into the wane he goes; \
And so will all but he that heart-work know.”]

So they went on, talking of what they had seen on the way, and in this way made easy a way that would no doubt otherwise have been tedious to them, for now they were going through a wilderness.
